Z||000003||Avery Montembeault||10-28-2001||10:12 PM||||try checking the relay. when my horn went, that was the problem||||reg||1
Z||000004||pHoOl||10-28-2001||11:12 PM||||I think that the relay still works, cause it still clicks.  But... i haven't checked anything else yet, cause it's cold and dark.  <P>From the article in Ogre's cave... it would sound like there are two horns?? why is this?  <P>------------------<BR>Kills:<BR>97 Neon R/T<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>         <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-28-2001||11:51 PM||||The majority of U.S. cars have 2 horns. They are usually adjusted so each one sounds a diferant note. The combined effect is louder and more penetrating than a single horn or a pair of horns on a single note. It's more complicated than just a straight DB number...<P>It works along the same lines as the old Civil Defence tone many people may remember. That tone was actually two notes carefully chosen to create the really irritating one that was braodcast. Needless to say, it was rare that it didn't get your attention from several rooms away when it went off. (God help you if you had headphone on when the sent it...)<P>Even when they are adjusted to the same note, as Meep Meeps sometimes are, they are louder than a single. <P>Plus you gain a backup. The two don't usually die at the same time unless the controls quit.<P>I think only 88 Fiero's have a single horn.<P>At least one horn will usually last the life of a car but it depends on a few things. In Fiero's case they are mounted in a fairly exposed low location. One dies but the driver often doesn't notice untill the other finally dies too. They aren't made to be submurged like they easily are in Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rick 88||10-29-2001||08:22 AM||||Check to see if you horn is properly grounded. If your relay is good then the horn may have failed. I backdated my 88 single horn to the better sounding dual 84 to 87's. There are plenty to be found in wrecking yards. The second horn bolts up in the stock location on the opposite side. All you have to do is run a wire from the new horn to the original horn lead. The earlier horns are higher quality and metal instead of plastic. Now my 88's horn has that "Fiero sound".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Rick 88||||reg||6
Z||000007||Formula88||10-29-2001||09:38 AM||||Thanks, Rick.  I was just about to ask what I need to do to add pre-88 horns to my Formula.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
